on  and that is the case so we will continue  to invest the same amount  into health and social care that we  would setting out through the levy but i  think it's important that we  make sure we now get into the detail of  how we are going to make improvements  for patients to access  ambulances or their doctors or dentists  indeed many other aspects of primary  care that's needed in order to i say  keep people um healthy  so general taxation to pay for health  and social care but of course is it  going to be general taxation is going to  pay for this um package of measures to  help households with their energy bills  or is that going to be borrowing  well we'll be going through that i mean  uh ultimately uh  we receive money through taxation from  uh from from people and businesses uh  and then redistribute that accordingly  to the priorities uh set out in our  manifesto and the needs of the country  so uh more will be said about that uh  aspects of that uh in during this week  i mean you're suggesting that you could  fund this through taxation that's not  the case that was it because this trust  is is not gonna fund this huge amount of  money that's gonna be spent through  taxation and it wouldn't get anywhere  near the amounts that are needed  well this is how the government receives  money from um  from people and businesses is through  general taxation and variety of ways  but it's important that we continue to  focus on honoring our manifesto in 2019  that's what the prime minister also said  uh on the doorstep of downing street and  that's what we intend to do  i'm conscious that we  have a lot to still achieve but we  should recognise that for example in the  nhs that there's more people working in  the nhs than ever before but also  recognize that there are still vacancies  too so we need to continue to get a grip  of  what is happening locally and i'm  looking forward to that challenge  i mean you forgive me for saying this  coffee but everyone knows that 